::STAT_U06_L07_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
Eight students are equally eligible to be chosen. What probability should each student have?

{
=A. 1/8#Correct. Equal chance among eight students gives each student probability 1/8.
~B. 1/4#This would be correct for four equal students, not eight.
~C. 1/2#This is too large because there are more than two eligible students.
~D. 8/1#A probability cannot be greater than 1.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
Which random number assignment is fair for four groups using numbers 1 to 100?

{
~A. A: 1-40, B: 41-60, C: 61-80, D: 81-100#Group A receives too many numbers.
=B. A: 1-25, B: 26-50, C: 51-75, D: 76-100#Correct. Each group receives 25 numbers.
~C. A: 1-10, B: 11-40, C: 41-70, D: 71-100#Group A receives fewer numbers than the others.
~D. A: 1-20, B: 21-45, C: 46-75, D: 76-100#The range sizes are not equal.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
Why is choosing the first volunteer usually not a fair random method?

{
~A. It uses too many numbers#The issue is not about numbers.
~B. It always chooses the wrong person#It may choose a person, but the method is biased.
=C. It favors people who volunteer quickly#Correct. Speed and confidence affect the outcome.
~D. It gives everyone exactly equal probability#The probabilities are not controlled or equal.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
Five names are written on identical slips and placed in a box. One slip is drawn without looking. What is each person's probability?

{
~A. 1/10#There are five slips, so 1/10 is too small.
~B. 1/4#This would fit four equally likely slips, not five.
~C. 5/1#This is greater than 1 and cannot be a probability.
=D. 1/5#Correct. One of five equally likely slips gives probability 1/5.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
A method is fair when all equally eligible outcomes have what?

{
=A. Equal probabilities#Correct. Fairness requires equal chances when outcomes are equally eligible.
~B. Different probabilities#Different probabilities would make some outcomes more likely.
~C. The same name#A fair method chooses among outcomes, not the same outcome every time.
~D. No chance of being selected#A fair selection gives eligible outcomes a chance.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q06::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
Six students are selected fairly using numbers 1 to 60. How many numbers should each student receive?

{
~A. 5#Six groups of 5 would only use 30 numbers.
=B. 10#Correct. 60 total numbers divided by 6 students gives 10 numbers each.
~C. 15#Six groups of 15 would require 90 numbers.
~D. 20#Six groups of 20 would require 120 numbers.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q07::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
Which method best supports fair selection?

{
~A. Choosing the person closest to the door#Seat location creates bias.
~B. Choosing the loudest person#Loudness creates bias.
=C. Assigning each person one number and using a random number generator#Correct. Equal number assignments support equal probability.
~D. Choosing the person with the highest grade#Grades do not create a random equal-chance method.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q08::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
A random number method assigns A: 1-50, B: 51-75, C: 76-100. What is wrong if A, B, and C should be equally likely?

{
~A. There are too few total numbers#There are enough numbers; the issue is unequal range size.
~B. C has no chance#C has numbers 76-100, so C has a chance.
~C. All groups have equal chances#They do not have equal chances because A has more numbers.
=D. A has more assigned numbers than B or C#Correct. More assigned numbers means a larger probability.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q09::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
If three outcomes are equally likely, what is the probability of each outcome?

{
=A. 1/3#Correct. Three equal outcomes split the total probability into thirds.
~B. 1/2#This would apply to two equal outcomes.
~C. 1/4#This would apply to four equal outcomes.
~D. 3/1#A probability cannot be greater than 1.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q10::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
Which statement best explains why identical slips matter in drawing lots?

{
~A. They make the result predictable#Identical slips make the draw less predictable, not predictable.
=B. They prevent the drawer from identifying a preferred slip#Correct. If slips can be distinguished, bias can enter the draw.
~C. They make every person lose#The goal is fair selection, not making everyone lose.
~D. They remove probability from the process#Drawing lots is a probability-based process.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q11::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
A fair method chooses one of 12 students. What should the probabilities add to?

{
~A. 12#Individual counts may be 12, but probabilities must total 1.
~B. 0#If probabilities add to 0, no outcome can happen.
=C. 1#Correct. The probabilities of all possible outcomes must add to 1.
~D. 2#Total probability cannot be 2.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q12::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
A teacher writes one student's name twice and every other name once before drawing. Why is this unfair?

{
~A. The repeated student has no chance#The repeated student has more chance, not no chance.
~B. Drawing slips can never be fair#Drawing slips can be fair if each eligible person has the same number of identical slips.
~C. The probabilities are all equal#The repeated name has a larger probability.
=D. One student has a larger chance of being selected#Correct. Extra slips increase that student's probability.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q13::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
Which assignment is fair for two teams using numbers 1 to 20?

{
=A. Team 1: 1-10, Team 2: 11-20#Correct. Each team receives 10 of the 20 numbers.
~B. Team 1: 1-15, Team 2: 16-20#Team 1 receives more numbers.
~C. Team 1: 1-5, Team 2: 6-20#Team 2 receives more numbers.
~D. Team 1: 1-12, Team 2: 13-20#The ranges are unequal.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q14::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
What is the main purpose of using probability in a fair decision?

{
~A. To guarantee one specific outcome#Probability does not guarantee a specific random outcome.
=B. To control the chances assigned to outcomes#Correct. Probability helps match the method to the intended chances.
~C. To remove all uncertainty#Random decisions still include uncertainty.
~D. To make calculations unnecessary#Fairness still requires checking probabilities.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q15::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
Four groups should not have equal chances. Group A should have probability 1/2, and B, C, and D should each have 1/6. What matters most?

{
~A. Every group must receive the same number of numbers#Equal numbers would not match the intended unequal probabilities.
~B. Group A must receive fewer numbers#Group A should receive more numbers because its probability is larger.
=C. The number assignments must match the intended probabilities#Correct. Fairness means matching the intended chance structure.
~D. Probabilities should add to 2#Probabilities for all outcomes should add to 1.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q16::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
A random number generator is set from 1 to 90 for three equal groups. Which range size should each group receive?

{
~A. 20#Three groups of 20 only account for 60 numbers.
~B. 25#Three groups of 25 only account for 75 numbers.
~C. 45#Three groups of 45 would require 135 numbers.
=D. 30#Correct. 90 divided by 3 equals 30 numbers per group.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q17::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
Which sentence is the best fairness explanation?

{
=A. Each eligible person has one identical slip, so each has the same probability.#Correct. This connects the method directly to equal probability.
~B. The method is fair because I like it.#Personal preference is not probability evidence.
~C. The method is fair because someone will win.#Having a winner does not prove equal chances.
~D. The method is fair because it is fast.#Speed does not prove fairness.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q18::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
If seven students are equally likely, each student's probability is closest to which decimal?

{
~A. 0.70#This is much too large for one of seven equal students.
=B. 0.143#Correct. 1/7 is approximately 0.143.
~C. 0.017#This is too small for one of seven equal students.
~D. 1.700#A probability cannot be greater than 1.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q19::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
Why might rolling a standard six-sided die be fair for choosing among six teams?

{
~A. The die has one side#A standard die has six sides.
~B. One team can choose its favorite number#That could introduce bias if not assigned fairly.
=C. Each team can be assigned one equally likely die result#Correct. Each die face has probability 1/6.
~D. The result is known before rolling#A random roll is not known before rolling.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q20::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
Which setup is unfair for four equal groups?

{
~A. Each group receives 25 numbers out of 100#This gives equal probabilities.
~B. Each group places one identical slip in a box#This gives equal probabilities.
~C. Each group is assigned one side of a fair four-section spinner#This gives equal probabilities if sections are equal.
=D. One group receives two slips and the others receive one slip#Correct. The group with two slips has a larger chance.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q21::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
What should you check first when judging whether a method is fair?

{
=A. Which outcomes are eligible and what probabilities they should have#Correct. Fairness depends on intended eligibility and probability.
~B. Which outcome you want to happen#Personal preference is not a fairness criterion.
~C. Whether the method is entertaining#Entertainment does not determine fair probability.
~D. Whether the result happened before#Past results do not by themselves determine whether the method is fair.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q22::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
In a fair equal selection among 20 students, how many students should have probability 1/20?

{
~A. 1#Only one student would not represent the whole class.
=B. 20#Correct. Each of the 20 students should have probability 1/20.
~C. 10#Ten students would leave half the class unaccounted for.
~D. 40#There are only 20 students in the selection.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q23::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
Which method creates bias?

{
~A. Randomly choosing from a complete list#A complete list with random selection supports fairness.
~B. Drawing identical slips with one slip per person#This supports equal probability.
~C. Assigning equal number ranges#Equal ranges support fairness for equal selection.
=D. Giving extra entries to one person without a stated reason#Correct. Extra entries increase that person's probability.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q24::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
If a random process is fair, does that mean the results will alternate perfectly among outcomes?

{
~A. Yes, fairness guarantees a perfect pattern#Randomness does not guarantee an alternating pattern.
~B. Yes, each outcome must appear once before repeating#Fairness does not require this kind of rotation.
=C. No, fairness means the probabilities are set correctly#Correct. Individual random results can still vary.
~D. No, because no one should ever be selected#A fair method selects someone while keeping probabilities appropriate.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_Q25::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
What is the best correction for an unfair equal-selection method?

{
=A. Give each eligible outcome the same number of equally likely chances#Correct. Equal chances are required for fair equal selection.
~B. Give the preferred outcome more chances#That would increase bias.
~C. Remove random selection completely#Removing randomness may not create equal chances.
~D. Ignore the probabilities#Fairness must be checked using probabilities.
}
